[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.1416","issue":"2025-08-10T00:55:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KTAE","awipsid":"AFDTAE"}]
=================================================



498

FXUS62 KTAE 100055

AFDTAE



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Tallahassee FL

855 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



...New UPDATE...



.UPDATE...

Issued at 853 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



Mostly updated rain chances through the rest of tonight and into

early Sunday morning. Scattered to widespread showers and

thunderstorms should begin to redevelop over our marine zones by

the early hours of Sunday morning before sunrise. 



&&



.NEAR TERM...

(Through Sunday)

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



A weak tropical wave is traversing the Florida Peninsula and

moving into the Gulf. This is leading to moderate low level 

easterly flow today, that will gradually shift to southeasterly by

Sunday afternoon. In the immediate term, ENE flow will continue 

to usher in abundant low cloudiness during the overnight hours, 

which will likely once again limit the onset of showers and 

thunderstorms on Sunday. Offshore convection, associated with the 

tropical wave, will also be important in how convective coverage 

for Sunday plays out. If there is too much offshore coverage 

tonight, then the cirrus canopy will overspread the region, 

slowing destabilization on Sunday and disrupting any sea breeze 

circulations. Nonetheless, the latest guidance, including the most

recent HRRR, suggests rather robust PoP coverage Sunday 

afternoon, especially in North Florida. To blend persistence with 

the latest model guidance, I did shift the onset of precipitation 

and the peak coverage a couple of hours later than the current 

guidance would suggest based on the trends observed over the last 

few days. And with all the cloudiness and expected convection, 

afternoon maxTs will be on the lower side, once again struggling 

to reach 90 degrees. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Sunday night through Tuesday night)

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



Mid-level shortwave and inverted surface trough reflection will 

traverse the region from southeast to northwest. This will pool 

anomalously high moisture with an axis of PWAT in excess of the 90th 

percentile, leading to above average chances of precip Monday and 

Tuesday. This translates into PoPs around 80% Monday (drier in the 

Southeast FL Big Bend) and up to ~70% Tuesday (highest west of the 

Ochlocknee and Flint Rivers). There is the potential for heavy 

rainfall on Monday and Tuesday, especially in aforementioned areas - 

See the hydrology section at the bottom for more details. Increased 

chances for PoPs will also lead to below average high temperatures, 

closer to the 25th percentile of guidance at best on both days.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Wednesday through Friday)

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



As the mid-level shortwave/surface trough alluded to in the short 

term section above continues moving west of the region, ridging will 

build in aloft. As heights build aloft and the 850 hPa flow becomes 

westerly, PoPs increasingly return to at or below normal between 

Wednesday and Friday. During this time frame, this results in heat 

building as high temps increase through the 90s, closer to the 75th 

percentile of guidance. While the westerly flow will also favor 

turbulent mixing of lower dew points to the surface from the late 

morning into the evening hours, the combination of heat and humidity

may lead to dangerous heat, although probably not as extensive as 

the last event. The probability of reaching the threshold of a Heat 

Advisory (Apparent Temperatures/Heat Indices AOA 108F is at least 

50% on Thursday and Friday - Stay tuned. WPC has also highlighted 

the potential for hazardous heat in their Day 3-7 Hazards Outlook 

for portions of the region. In addition, drier air aloft From 

Wednesday through Friday may lead to greater wind gust potential for 

afternoon and evening thunderstorms.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 717 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



Some light SHRA will pass near TLH and VLD during the next couple

hours, but most of these SHRA are weakening. Otherwise, VFR

conditions should continue at all sites for the next 3-6 hours. A

stratus deck will move in late this evening, beginning at VLD and

ABY, spreading SW. MVFR to IFR cigs are likely at all sites

tonight with some signal for LIFR cigs away from ECP. For now,

have started the trend toward a SCT LIFR deck. Cigs will be slow

to rise tomorrow with MVFR continuing through about 18z at most

spots. Scattered to numerous SHRA/TSRA will redevelop after 18z

near most sites. Generally E/NE winds around 5-10 kt continue.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



Continued easterly flow this weekend with a frontal boundary south 

of the waters. As the frontal boundary lifts north of the waters on 

Monday, the flow will clock around to southerly into Tuesday, then 

to more southwesterly Wednesday and Thursday. With a weak pressure 

gradient in place, winds will tend to maximize near the coast in the 

seabreeze zone during the afternoon and evening hours. Showers and 

thunderstorms will be most widespread between Sunday and Tuesday, 

with waterspouts possible. Briefly higher winds and seas are likely 

in the vicinity of any thunderstorms.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



Low fire weather concerns are expected for the next few days with 

moist conditions and increasing rain chances. The wettest locations 

will likely be across the Florida Big Bend with lower rain chances 

across southeast Alabama.



&&



.HYDROLOGY...

Issued at 117 PM EDT Sat Aug 9 2025



The potential for heavy rainfall will increase from east to west 

across the region through at least Monday. For Saturday afternoon 

and evening, there is a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) of excessive 

rain mainly in the eastern FL Big Bend. Portions of this area east 

of the Aucilla River observed 300-400% of normal rainfall over the 

past week, with a low chance of isolated flash flooding. On Sunday, 

much of the region generally east of the Apalachicola and Flint 

Rivers has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) of excessive rain, with 

CAMs favoring the FL Big Bend coastal areas for heaviest rainfall,  

which would be the least flash flood prone. On Monday, much of the

region has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) of excessive rain with the 

most vulnerable locations for flash flooding generally north of GA-

37 and GA-91, where heavier rainfall occurred over the past week.

But the heavier rainfall may again remain just south of this area, 

especially in the FL Big Bend. There is also a signal on Monday

in the CAMs for heavier rainfall to get close to the I-10 corridor

of the FL Big Bend, such as a Tallahassee, so an increased risk 

of flash flooding is possible. Cannot rule out heavy rainfall on' 

Tuesday in the FL Panhandle and Southeast AL, with a downward trend 

over the remainder of the region. For mid to late week, a more 

typical summer pattern with at or below average chances of diurnal 

precipitation each day. 



On the rivers, portions of the Kinchafoonee Creek remains in action 

stage. Looking ahead, there are no river flooding concerns over the 

next week.



&&



.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...



Spotter activation is not requested. However, spotters are always 

encouraged to safely report significant weather conditions when they 

occur by calling the office or tweeting us @NWSTallahassee.



&&



